course: dictionary
lesson: 1128
title: The breastbone: one bone, two places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The breastbone is the long, flat bone in the middle of your chest. The ribs join onto it at the front, and together they make a cage that guards your heart and lungs.
explain: Its scientific name is the sternum, so in science lessons you may see that word instead. You may also hear the phrase «behind the breastbone», which means the space just inside the middle of your chest.
explain: The word is not only for people. «A bird's strong flight muscles are fixed to a keel on its breastbone.» The word keel is new, but the sentence gives clues: the keel is on the breastbone, and the big muscles are fixed to it. A keel is a ridge, so the flight muscles hang from a ridge on the bird's breastbone.
fact: The breastbone is also called the sternum.
fact: The breastbone and the ribs together protect the heart and lungs.
fact: In first aid, chest compressions in CPR are pressed on the lower half of the breastbone.
fact: In a bird, «breastbone» means the same bone, and the flight muscles are fixed to it.
fact: «Coiled» means wound round in loops, like a spring.
exercise: «A bird's strong flight muscles are fixed to a keel on its breastbone.» Where are the flight muscles fixed? | Around the eyes | To the tail feathers | To the beak | *To a keel on the breastbone
exercise: The breastbone has another name in science. What is it? | Pelvis | *Sternum | Skull | Femur
exercise: «A crane's windpipe is very long and coiled inside its breastbone like a trumpet's tube.» Find and copy one word that means wound round in loops. | windpipe | *coiled | breastbone | trumpet
test: What is the breastbone also called? | Spine | Shoulder blade | *Sternum | Collarbone
test: In CPR, where are the chest compressions pressed? | On the top of the head | On the stomach | On the back of the neck | *On the lower half of the breastbone
exam: «A crane's windpipe is very long and coiled inside its breastbone like a trumpet's tube, so its call is loud and deep.» Why is the crane's call so loud and deep? | It shouts with its mouth closed | Its beak is very big | Its ribs are hollow | *Its windpipe is long and coiled inside its breastbone
